{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in modern filmmaking, a sharp, tightly wound drama that dissects the birth of facebook and the corrosive nature of ambition, betrayal, and isolation in the digital age. directed by david fincher with surgical precision, the film moves at a relentless pace, its dialogue crackling with wit and tension, courtesy of aaron sorkin’s razor-sharp screenplay. the story follows mark zuckerberg, portrayed with chilling nuance by jesse eisenberg, as he transforms from a brilliant but socially awkward harvard student into the enigmatic founder of the world’s most influential social platform.\n\neisenberg’s performance is a revelation — he captures zuckerberg’s intelligence, insecurity, and emotional detachment with such subtlety that the character becomes both sympathetic and infuriating. the supporting cast is equally stellar: andrew garfield brings heart and vulnerability as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s loyal best friend turned adversary, while jesse plemons and armie hammer deliver unforgettable turns as the winklevoss twins, embodying entitlement and righteous fury in equal measure. the film’s legal framing device — two parallel lawsuits — adds structure and depth, allowing sorkin to explore themes of ownership, originality, and the cost of innovation.\n\ndanny elfman’s score, with its pulsing electronic motifs, perfectly mirrors the film’s digital heartbeat, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, minimalist soundtrack elevates every scene into something almost prophetic. the cinematography, by jeff cronenweth, is cold and clean, reflecting the sterile world of code and corporate power, yet the emotional landscapes are anything but empty. every frame feels deliberate, every pause loaded with meaning.\n\nwhat makes the social network so enduring isn’t just its portrayal of a tech revolution — it’s its portrayal of human failure. it’s about how connection, in the age of social media, can become the very thing that isolates. it’s about friendship shattered by jealousy, genius corrupted by pride, and the quiet tragedy of someone who built a global community but lost the ability to belong to one.\n\nthis is not a biopic in the traditional sense; it’s a psychological portrait wrapped in a legal thriller, a cautionary tale dressed as a drama. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg — it dissects him. and in doing so, it holds up a mirror to all of us, asking: what are we willing to sacrifice for success? the answer, as the film so elegantly shows, might be everything.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a great movie — it’s a cultural landmark. it captures a moment in time, yes, but more importantly, it captures the soul of a generation.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.371579885482788}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " modern", "logprob": -1.516965627670288}, {"token": " filmm", "logprob": -1.2380247116088867}, {"token": "aking", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.10196460038423538}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.37716901302337646}, {"token": " sharp", "logprob": -0.6199471354484558}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.8609820219571702e-05}, {"token": " tightly", "logprob": -1.8686988353729248}, {"token": " wound", "logprob": -0.016664354130625725}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.017919549718499184}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0017234723782166839}, {"token": " dis", "logprob": -1.4531874656677246}, {"token": "sects", "logprob": -3.7788631743751466e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1385976076126099}, 